aphor","back":"Describes one thing as another to suggest a meaningful resemblance.","hint":"“The hallway was a river of backpacks.”","explanation":"","tags":["language","literature","beginner"],"aiGenerated":true}]},"publishRequest":{"operationId":"2e8f4c5d-283c-505e-9b70-32074fa3c0eb","deckId":"caff9062-c6dc-5ae5-918b-7f33be1001d5","rightsAccepted":true,"expectedContentHash":"39b1a02cc6ea5c1d6dac7e38860a3480aac67b3ab51111ec7dfc30a8f8cc86e2","showDownloadCount":false,"showUsageStats":false},"payloadHash":"bcb0103c7708a7bb6f2d714da53bee6b837bd073f484e201cc1b9f002b21c6fd"},{"slug":"music-fundamentals","deck":{"id":"3e8b738d-2ba2-5d7a-835b-cb8d5153eb7c","name":"Music fundamentals","description":"A Recollect AI starter in Western music notation: pitch, beats, scales, and the building blocks of chords.\n\nSources:\nPuget Sound — Pitch: https://musictheory.pugetsound.edu/mt21c/Pitch.html\nPuget Sound — Meter: https://musictheory.pugetsound.edu/mt21c/meter.html\nPuget Sound — Half Steps and Whole Steps: https://musictheory.pugetsound.edu/mt21c/HalfStepsAndWholeSteps.html\nPuget Sound — The Major Scale: https://musictheory.pugetsound.edu/mt21c/TheMajorScale.html\nPuget Sound — Introduction to Intervals: https://musictheory.pugetsound.edu/mt21c/IntervalsIntroduction.html\nPuget Sound — Introduction to Triads: https://musictheory.pugetsound.edu/mt21c/TriadsIntroduction.html\nPuget Sound — Durational Symbols: https://musictheory.pugetsound.edu/mt21c/DurationalSymbols.html","color":"#BC9652","icon":"🎵","isSilenced":false,"silencedUntil":null,"ownerId":"8f9f97b2-60da-5803-9db9-7067a80f9203","version":1,"createdAt":"2026-10-26T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-10-26T00:00:00.000Z","deletedAt":null},"cards":[{"id":"b7b51aee-c9ce-55eb-a0b0-31c3c88cea4d","deckId":"3e8b738d-2ba2-5d7a-835b-cb8d5153eb7c","front":"Pitch","back":"How high or low a musical sound seems.","hint":"Compare the left and right ends of a piano.","explanation":"","tags":["music","beginner"],"aiGenerated":true,"own